  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "In 27 BCE, one of the two giant stone statues suddenly started singing every morning. Tourists sailed down the Nile just to catch the eerie chime echoing across the desert dawn.",
        "visual_query": "luxor egyptian desert monuments",
        "visual_fallback": "ancient stone statues"
      },
      {
        "narration": "A massive earthquake had just shattered the northern statue, cracking it from waist to crown. Ever since the rubble settled, the wounded giant greeted the sunrise with a clear, haunting note.",
        "visual_query": "weathered sandstone temple ruins",
        "visual_fallback": "cracked stone ruin"
      },
      {
        "narration": "For centuries, the sound baffled everyone who heard it. Was it a trapped spirit? Roman emperors carved Latin poetry into the boots to honor the musical god.",
        "visual_query": "ancient egyptian hieroglyphic carving",
        "visual_fallback": "carved stone wall"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Then, a Roman emperor tried to fix the damage. In 199 CE, workers stacked new sandstone blocks to patch the fractured torso, accidentally silencing the desert forever.",
        "visual_query": "ancient roman sandstone architecture",
        "visual_fallback": "stone blocks ruin"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The morning sun heating moisture inside the cracked, porous stone had been the source all along. The repair work altered the temperature, permanently turning off the world's most famous acoustic illusion.",
        "visual_query": "sunrise over desert ruins",
        "visual_fallback": "desert sunrise landscape"
      }
    ],
